  "summary": "Times of San Diego analyzes the geographic distribution of the city's 505 city-owned ALPR cameras by community and police beat, finding the highest concentrations in Southeastern San Diego, Downtown, and Encanto. The piece is pegged to a Sept. 30 ACLU and Partnership for the Advancement of New Americans court petition seeking to force San Diego to end its ALPR program on privacy grounds.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "On Sept. 30 the ACLU Foundation and Partnership for the Advancement of New Americans filed a petition in court to force the city of San Diego to end its use of automated license plate reader technology.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Nearly 20% of the city's share of cameras are in two communities: Southeastern San Diego, which has 54 cameras, and Downtown, which has 42 cameras.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Out of 52 communities in San Diego, there are 15 without city-owned ALPRs.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    }
  ],
